  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Today through Friday Fog & Low Cloud Potential: Strong high \n   pressure increasing likelihood of valley inversions.\n\n-  Pattern Shift: A Pacific system will tap into atmospheric river\n   moisture beginning Saturday, snow levels stay relatively high\n   but precipitation amounts very light.\n\n-  Further cooling early to mid next week with potential for snow\n   levels to lower further.\n\n\nToday through Friday: High pressure builds into the Northern \nRockies today and will persist through Friday, setting up strong \nvalley inversions and stagnant conditions. The primary forecast \nchallenge over the next few days will be the extent and duration \nof fog and low stratus. Recent precipitation has left abundant \nlow-level moisture across most of the area, with the exception of \nLemhi County and southwest Montana. Therefore, as areas of partial\nclearing occur this morning, areas of valley fog will likely \ndevelop. If this fog becomes deeply entrenched it may be stubborn \nto mix out, though most guidance suggests enough warming and dry \nair aloft to dissipate the majority of it by midday.\n\nLooking ahead to tonight and Thursday night, incoming high clouds\ncould help limit new fog formation. However, strong radiational \ncooling is still favored, meaning valley fog is likely to return \neach night. Additionally, with Flathead Lake surface temperatures \nstill around 50F, the cold, stable airmass will likely allow the \nLake to fuel stratus development across the Flathead and Mission \nvalleys (and potential even more widespread). Finally, if fog and \nstratus become more widespread or persistent than expected, \ndaytime valley high temperatures will likely fall short of current\nforecasts.\n\nSaturday through Next Week: The next chance for precipitation \narrives Saturday morning as southwest flow establishes itself and \na decaying atmospheric river clips the Northern Rockies. A weak \ncold front will cross the region, but models have trended drier \nwith what was already expected to be a light precipitation event.  \nCurrent ensembles show less than a tenth of an inch of liquid \nequivalent, even in typically favored wetter locations. While snow\nlevels will hover between 5,500 and 6,000 feet, winter weather \nimpacts will be negligible, with only a dusting of snow expected \nover mountain passes.\n\nFor next week, ensemble guidance strongly favors a split-flow \npattern. While successive upper-level troughs and atmospheric \nrivers will impact the West Coast, the bulk of the dynamic energy \nand moisture will dive south into California and the Desert \nSouthwest. For the Northern Rockies, this translates to generally \nlight precipitation and a return to seasonable temperatures (which\nwill feel notably colder, roughly 10 to 15 degrees cooler than \nour recent mild stretch). Mountain passes may see occasional light\nsnow, but widespread or significant travel impacts are not\nsupported by the majority of ensemble clusters. One thing to \nwatch, about 25% of ensemble clusters support a weak closed low \nTuesday, this would allow for moderate pass level snow impacts, \nstay tuned!",
